WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.420
This morning President-elect Donald Trump

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:06.440
officially named his pick to lead the environmental
protection agency,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.050
Oklahoma Attorney General Scott Pruitt.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.480
The swearing in ceremony took place in the
White House Treaty Room

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.420
with Supreme Court Justice Samuel Alito administering
the oath.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.520
Trump’s EPA chief came into office determined
to end what he dubbed

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.210
the agency’s “anti-energy agenda,”

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.960
by loosening requirements on carbon emissions
and eliminating land use restrictions.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.550
Pruitt has set out to transform the agency
he leads

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:35.329
perhaps to a greater extent than any of Trump’s
other cabinet appointees.

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:41.800
We as an agency, and we as a nation, can be
both pro-energy and jobs and pro-environment.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.760
One of his biggest targets:

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.720
a controversial rule on water pollution put
in place by the Obama administration.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.699
You had a power grab that was taking place
at the EPA

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:54.800
where they were trying to assert jurisdiction
over land use decisions

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.980
that were being made by private property owners

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.240
and cities and towns in states across the country.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.140
Progressives were outraged.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.560
He is going to dismantle everything in place.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.180
The air you breathe, the water you drink.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.420
To understand why property rights advocates
applauded Pruitt’s move,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.920
consider the case of fourth-generation farmer
John Duarte,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.720
who has fought a protracted and costly legal
battle with federal regulators

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.580
over how to till his 450 acre farm in Tehama
County, California.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.260
If the government can do this to us

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.580
there’s no family in America who can farm
their land

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.640
without concern the government can come on
to their property

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.020
and take everything they own.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.240
My family’s farming history goes back to
my great grandfathers.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.780
We produce grapevines, almond trees, pistachio
trees, walnuts, avocados,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.860
and a few other minor crops.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.420
The government is saying that our tillage
of this ground to plant a wheat crop

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.320
violated the Clean Water Act.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:58.060
In 2012 the Army Corps of Engineers, working
in conjunction with the EPA,

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.600
accused Duarte of damaging wetland features
on his property.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.040
The penalty: over $30 million in fines and
restoration fees.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.060
But getting permission before plowing wasn’t
really an option.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:16.150
The average time to obtain a Clean Water Act
permit is close to two years.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:19.030
And the average cost just to hire the consultants

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.680
and do the studies to get permits approaches
a quarter of a million dollars.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.950
Attorney Anthony Francois with the Pacific
Legal Foundation

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.040
represented Duarte in his case against the
government.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.250
Clearly if you had to undertake that kind
of cost and time

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.480
just to get the necessary permit to plow your
fields every year

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.480
you’re not going to grow a lot of food.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.760
Duarte’s troubles stemmed from a provision
in the Clean Water Act

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.420
known as the Waters of the United States rule

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.020
that was meant to better protect large bodies of water

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.280
by regulating use of the streams, ponds, and
ditches that flow into them.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.980
But in a power grab by agency bureaucrats,

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.340
Obama’s EPA expanded the power granted by
this provision,

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:05.460
using it as license to micromanage private
land use to the point of absurdity.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:11.000
The agency accused Duarte of mismanaging the
wetland areas located on his property,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.800
claiming that his four inch plow furrows created
small mountain ranges.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.200
Not those mountains, these ones.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.420
That’s what we have right here, in these
furrows.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:27.600
They’re saying the bottom of this furrow
is still a wetland.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.960
But the top of this furrow—five inches higher—is
a converted upland.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.349
Attorney generals from 31 states, including
Pruitt,

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:40.490
challenged the Obama administration’s overreach
on the Clean Water Act.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.120
The broad trend of environmental regulation
during the Obama administration

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.150
was to use the coercive threat or reality
of regulation

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:54.000
simply to try to shut down entire industries
and entire types of economic activity.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.820
That’s University of Virginia law professor
Jason Scott Johnston.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.459
He says that the Obama administration expanded
the definition of wetlands

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:05.820
beyond the parameters set by the Supreme Court
in a 2007 decision.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.820
They have promulgated a definition of wetlands

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.900
which clearly contradicts what the Supreme Court said.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.080
They're going to go out and regulate things
that they consider to be wetlands,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.000
even though this Court has said they're not.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.620
EPA’s so-called Waters of the United States
rule

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:24.880
is one of the worst examples of federal regulation
and it has truly run amok.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.780
The Trump Administration has started the process
of repealing the rule,

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:34.000
but faces multiple legal hurdles and getting
it off the books could take several years.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.520
Faced with millions of dollars in fines,

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.080
Duarte settled his case in August for $1.1
million.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:45.310
He hopes Pruitt’s focus on regulatory rollback
will restore the property rights of farmers.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:50.020
We become peasants where these federal prosecutors
can come in like the Sheriff of Nottingham,

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.280
decide for themselves what they think a family
can pay,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.000
decide for themselves if they want to destroy
a family and take their land away.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.960
If the federal prosecutors can come on this
farm with this set of facts

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.680
there is no farm in America that is safe from
this kind of prosecution.

